   479	Lemma 5.3. Suppose that N > 2, l = LF (see §2) is irreducible, and µl f = N − 1.
   480	                                 def
   481	Then the restriction of f to l′ = l − L∞ is a biregular isomorphism, and hence, the
   482	curve f (l′ ) is nonsingular in C2 .
   483	Proof. The mapping f |l′ separates points. Indeed, if f (x) = f (y) for distinct points
   484	x, y ∈ l′ , then µx f ∗ + µy f ∗ = 2N − 2 > N which contradicts the condition (2) of
   485	constant multiplicity of f . Hence, first, f (l′ ) is nonsingular since it may not have
   486	irreducible singularities by Lemma 5.2, and second, f |l′ is an isomorphism. The
   487	lemma is proved.
   488	   In order to prove Theorem 1.1, we shall show that the multiplicity N of f ∗ (see
   489	§4) cannot equal two or three. The fact that N 6= 2 was proved in §1, but we may
   490	also derive it as an immediate corollary of Lemma 4.3. If N = 2 then, by Corollary
   491	4.3 the multiplicity of f ∗ at every point of π(LF ) except the point ∞ is equal to
   492	one, hence, f ∗ is a two-fold unbranched covering of C2 , which is a contradiction.
   493	   Suppose that N = 3. Then by Lemma 4.2 three cases may occur: 1) LF consists
   494	of two components and µx f ∗ = 1 for all x ∈ π(LF ) − {∞}; 2) LF is irreducible
   495	and µx f ∗ = 1 for all x ∈ π(LF ) − {∞, x0 }, where x0 6= ∞; or 3) LF is irreducible
