+### 5.3 Degree four: published Lemma 9
+
+There are two factorizations.
+
+For \((m,n)=(2,2)\), the three raw rows have a split \(2+2\) in one of
+\(L,R,D\) and label 4 in the other directions.  A split on the left or below
+would repeat a nonunit determinant.  The only survivor of that first filter
+is
+
+\[
+4\mid22\mid4,                                           \tag{5.4}
+\]
+
+Russian Fig. 19.  Its left endpoint cannot end, since the left and lower
+labels are both even; it is a fork of transverse degree two.  But repeating
+the same classification at that endpoint requires its own right side to
+contain an unsplit degree-four arm, whereas (5.4) provides two degree-two
+arms.  The tree and (4.1) leave no hidden arm.  Thus the \((2,2)\) factorization
+is impossible.
+
+For \((4,1)\), determinant pruning can be replayed without trusting the
+pictures.  An endpoint block with partition \(1+1+1+1\) or \(2+1+1\) repeats
+a nonunit determinant and dies.  A \(3+1\) block forces its target branch
+determinant to be 3, since the two coprime transfer factors are \(P/3,P\).
+A \(2+2\) block forces it to be 2.  The target left and lower determinants
+are coprime by Proposition 3.  Consequently:
+
+- with one left arm, that arm is 4, the lower partition must be \(3+1\), and
+  the right partition is \(2+1+1\): Russian Fig. 13;
+- with at least two left arms, the allowed triples are
+  \(31\mid31\mid22\), \(31\mid22\mid31\),
+  \(31\mid22\mid22\), \(22\mid31\mid31\), and
+  \(22\mid31\mid22\): Russian Figs. 14--18 (with the source's direction
+  convention).
+
+That is six candidates, and the preceding factor analysis accounts for all
